{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmA7vnnFzktydqKPv6Tm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the repaired candidate, and it passes the self-check (`OK`). Whether it now converges is still unknown. The self-check doesn't test that, and I didn't run a multi-chain fit to find out.\n\n**What I changed and why.** The model is still a refinement of the incumbent `short_sequence_pseudoflip_dilution`, with one change: a person's impression of how much a sequence switches grows with diminishing returns. The first one or two breaks in a streak make a sequence look much more random, and each further switch adds less. This targets the critique finding that survived the false-discovery correction: in long pairs with few switches, people reward the first breaks of a streak more than the incumbent predicts.\n\nThe previous attempt failed admission on convergence (R-hat 1.53, bulk ESS 7). My diagnosis: its curve `((k+1)^rho − 1)/rho` changed its overall size along with its shape. That let `rho` trade off against each person's switch weight `w_i`. I pinned the curve at two points so `rho` can only bend it, not resize it:\n\n`f(k) = 3·((k+1)^rho − 1)/(4^rho − 1)`, so `f(0) = 0` and `f(3) = 3` for every `rho`.\n\nAll of the overall strength now sits with `w_i`. With `rho = 1` the curve is a straight line, which is the incumbent's version. I also narrowed the prior on `log_rho` from sigma 0.5 to 0.4.\n\n**Self-check result.** The check (1 chain, 100 tuning + 100 draws) gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −3562.9; the incumbent's is −3566.8. That number comes from a very short fit, so it doesn't show the candidate is better. The check also gave two warnings:\n- **R-hat:** a single chain can't report one, so convergence wasn't verified.\n- **PSIS-LOO:** marked unreliable, because 7% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5 against a 1% tolerance.\n\nThe first is a real gap: this attempt was rejected for not converging, and admission's fit needs R-hat ≤ 1.05 and bulk ESS ≥ 100. I didn't run a fuller fit because there's only one CPU here; the smoke fit alone took about 8 minutes, so a two-chain test would have taken an estimated 1.5 hours or more. My reasoning that pinning the curve fixes the problem is untested. One case I can't rule out: if people's average switch weight is close to zero, the data barely constrain `rho`, and it would rest mostly on its prior. This is the last attempt for this slot, so another rejection is final. Novelty against the live models also wasn't checked.\n\n**Name.** I renamed the model `anchored_concave_switch_impression`.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_3_repair_1/`:\n- hypothesis.md\n- model_name.txt\n- candidate.py"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1005,"cache_read_input_tokens":108016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1005,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":761,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c94963a-3a24-4b79-945e-e90a44e74baf","uuid":"4b5a2498-a005-4d87-9ddd-f796907812b6","timestamp":"2026-10-06T14:41:21.332Z","request_id":"req_011CfmA7vQhr7tbXbC6NVREC"}
